---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevEncodeFancyText from '@/components/tools/text/DevEncodeFancyText.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="strikethrough-text-generator">
  <DevEncodeFancyText
    client:load
    sample="crossed out"
    featured={['strikethrough', 'strikethrough-short', 'slashed', 'underline', 'double-underline']}
  />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How strikethrough works in plain text</h2>
    <p>
      Unicode has a category of characters called combining marks. They have no width; they attach to the
      character before them, and the font draws both together. The strikethrough text generator puts U+0336
      COMBINING LONG STROKE OVERLAY after each letter, so the line is part of the text and needs no
      formatting.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Mark</th><th>Codepoint</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Long stroke overlay</td><td>U+0336</td><td>The standard strikethrough</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Short stroke overlay</td><td>U+0335</td><td>Shorter dashes; better in wide fonts</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Long solidus overlay</td><td>U+0338</td><td>A diagonal slash through each character</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Low line</td><td>U+0332</td><td>Underline</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Double low line</td><td>U+0333</td><td>Double underline</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Overline</td><td>U+0305</td><td>A line above</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Why the line sometimes looks broken</h2>
    <p>
      The font draws each mark per character, so the line is a row of short strokes. Whether they join
      depends on the font. Monospace and condensed fonts join cleanly; wide proportional fonts leave visible
      gaps, most of all around spaces and narrow letters such as <code>i</code> and <code>l</code>. If the
      standard mark has gaps, try the short-stroke or slash variant. The slash variant never looks broken,
      because it is not meant to be continuous.
    </p>

    <h2>Use the native syntax where it exists</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Where</th><th>Native way</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Discord</td><td>Wrap in double tildes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Slack</td><td>Wrap in single tildes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>WhatsApp</td><td>Wrap in single tildes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Markdown / GitHub</td><td>Double tildes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Google Docs, Word</td><td>A real strikethrough command</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Native formatting renders consistently and stays searchable. Use this page for bios, form fields,
      spreadsheet cells, plain-text notes and apps with no formatting.
    </p>

    <h2>Search stops finding the words</h2>
    <p>
      A combining mark changes the byte sequence, so a plain-text search for the original word no longer
      finds it. This can be useful, for a crossed-out phrase that must not appear in search results, or a
      problem, when you later search for your own note. Keep this in mind before you strike through a whole
      document.
    </p>

    <h2>How screen readers read it</h2>
    <p>
      Usually badly. Some screen readers ignore combining marks and read the text normally, some announce
      each mark, and some spell the word out. A real strikethrough in a document is announced as such, and
      the listener can skip it; this text cannot be skipped. If the strikethrough has a meaning, such as a
      price that no longer applies or a finished task, also say so in words.
    </p>

    <h2>Copying safely</h2>
    <p>
      Combining marks are invisible characters, so a partial selection can leave a stray mark or lose one at
      the start. Use the copy button next to each style, most of all on a phone.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
